Hydrocephalus
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Ventriculomegaly, Ataxic/Magnetic gait, Urinary incontinence

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-019
Reason for Visit: Sudden, severe morning headache with unprovoked vomiting and dramatic worsening of mobility in an elderly patient.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute exacerbation of severe, generalized morning headaches associated with projectile vomiting and a sudden inability to walk. Urgent assessment is mandatory to rule out acute hydrocephalic decompensation or shunt malfunction, which poses an immediate threat to life via brainstem herniation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My head is splitting open, especially when I lie flat, and I couldn't even stand up to make it to the bathroom today."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a severe, generalized headache that has worsened over the last week, but today is consistently worse in the morning and explicitly worsened when lying supine. This is associated with explosive, unprovoked nausea and forceful vomiting today. The spouse notes a recent, insidious onset of progressive cognitive decline and lethargy. There is a distinct gait ataxia, commonly presenting as a wide-based, magnetic, or shuffling gait, which today has deteriorated to an inability to stand. The patient has also developed new urinary incontinence presenting as severe urgency leading to completely uninhibited bladder emptying over the last 48 hours.
Functional Status: Acutely bedbound; unable to mobilize safely.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of falls; impending neurological catastrophe.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 165/95 mmHg | HR: 58 bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 14 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Lethargic, holding head, actively nauseated, lying flat in bed.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination reveals a lethargic but rousable patient. Fundoscopic examination reveals bilateral papilledema signifying severely elevated intracranial pressure. Cranial nerve exam shows a mild upward gaze palsy. Tone is increased in the lower extremities. Hyperreflexia and distinct spasticity predominantly affecting the lower extremities are noted (3+ reflexes at knees, upgoing plantars bilaterally). When assisted to stand, the patient exhibits a severely ataxic, wide-based, magnetic gait, unable to lift feet from the floor. Diagnostic tetrad features are clinically evident (magnetic gait, incontinence, signs of elevated ICP suggesting ventriculomegaly).
Environmental Observations: Vomit basin by the bed; urine-soaked sheets.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications known to cause acute intracranial hypertension.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Hydrocephalus (Suspected Acute Decompensation / Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus progression) - Status: Critical Exacerbation
Differentials:

Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symmetrical, bilateral lower limb involvement, profound signs of globally elevated intracranial pressure (papilledema, morning vomiting), and lack of cortical deficits like aphasia or hemianopia.

Parkinson's Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of hyperreflexia/spasticity (upper motor neuron signs), true papilledema, and early, severe urinary incontinence.

Urinary Tract Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile status, and UTI cannot explain the papilledema, severe morning headaches, and profound upper motor neuron signs in the legs.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic Hakim-Adams triad (cognitive decline, urinary incontinence, magnetic gait) acutely complicated by alarming signs of critically elevated intracranial pressure (morning headaches, explosive vomiting, papilledema, and Cushing's reflex of hypertension/bradycardia). This indicates a transition from chronic to acute hydrocephalus, representing a neurosurgical emergency. Delaying assessment until morning would risk fatal tonsillar herniation. Urgent after-hours assessment definitively recognized the signs of critical intracranial hypertension and the specific hydrocephalic gait, mandating immediate emergency medical transport for urgent neuroimaging and life-saving cerebrospinal fluid diversion (e.g., external ventricular drain or shunt revision).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate dispatch of emergency ambulance for ED transfer. Elevated the head of the bed to 30-45 degrees to optimize cerebral venous outflow. Administered Metoclopramide 10mg IM to manage severe emesis.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Step 1: By strictly maintaining the head of the bed elevated with pillows, the patient actively utilizes gravity to drain blood from the brain, immediately reducing the dangerous pressure inside the skull.
Step 2: By remaining entirely in bed and refusing to stand, the patient prevents a catastrophic fall caused by the severe magnetic gait and leg spasticity.
Step 3: By avoiding coughing, straining, or holding their breath, the patient prevents sudden spikes in chest pressure, which directly prevents further acute elevations in brain pressure.
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A - Hospital admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Handover to tertiary hospital emergency and neurosurgery teams.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance is en route. If the patient becomes completely unresponsive or begins to have a seizure, roll them onto their side (recovery position) and ensure the airway is clear of vomit.

